# Open SWE on Managed Deep Agents

Open SWE's coding agent, pull request reviewer and review scout, packaged as one [Managed Deep Agents](https://docs.langchain.com/langsmith/python/managed-deep-agents-overview) project with a single graph, `open-swe`. Each run's context names the Open SWE graph it plays (`agent`, `reviewer` or `review-scout`); the factory in `agent.py` builds that graph's deep agent from it.

The model loop, its checkpoints, every tool call and the middleware around them run here. The backend works out each run's catalog as it starts the run (`openswe/agent_api/graph.py`): its operations, its models and the rules of its middleware. Every call MDA makes on the backend, the loop's and the tools', is a named endpoint of the typed API in `packages/runtime-api` (`runtime_api`), under `POST /agent-api/{endpoint}`, which both sides import, answered from the run token and the request on whichever backend replica it lands. Each endpoint declares how long it may take and when it is sent again after a failed delivery. Every call is authenticated with the run token.

- **Sandbox:** MDA names, creates, adopts, restarts and replaces each thread's LangSmith box, one per thread. The catalog gives the options it is created with (the workspace snapshot, VM size, TTLs, `preserve_memory_on_stop`), which apply only when MDA creates it, so a refreshed snapshot never strands a thread's work. Before `prepare`, the run opens the box (`open_sandbox`) and reports its name; the backend binds the thread to it (`sandbox_owner: mda`) and connects as to any reused box: proxy credentials, git identity, tool channel. MDA leaves the proxy config to the backend (`_openswe_no_manage_proxy=True`). `recreate_sandbox` on such a box comes back from the backend as what to replace it with; MDA replaces it under the same name (`replace_sandbox`, the old box stopped and kept renamed), and the backend reconnects through `sandbox_replaced`. The backend never creates or replaces an MDA-owned box. A tool MDA runs that finds the box unreachable ends the run, and the backend tells the person through `sandbox_unreachable`. Threads the backend gave a box keep it; bridged threads run on the user's machine; a task worker works in its coordinator's MDA box once the backend has attached it there.
- **Browser:** the coding agent (`agent`) also gets Stagehand's `browser_run`, `browser_snapshot` and `browser_screenshot` tools on one LangSmith browser per thread (`managed_deepagents.browser`), which shares the thread's sandbox's `localhost`, and `browser_record`, which records the browser's tab as a video saved in the sandbox (carried in the vendored SDK as managed-deepagents-sdk#1010). The deployment's workspace needs the LangSmith browser beta. It has no `browser_handoff`: MDA's watcher run would start the run that wakes the agent when the user hands the browser back, and Open SWE runs only what its backend signs (`rt/browser.py`).
